WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:03.689
Let's take a quick look at using Cloud SQL
with App Engine.

00:00:03.689 --> 00:00:05.920
The first step is to create a database.

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.110
Go to the SQL section and click create instance
and choose "second generation."

00:00:10.110 --> 00:00:14.440
Give your instance a name, and then choose
the region and zone for your instance.

00:00:14.440 --> 00:00:19.020
You can now change the instance type and the
storage capacity.

00:00:19.020 --> 00:00:21.020
Don't worry, you can change these later if
you want to.

00:00:21.020 --> 00:00:26.189
You can also ensure high availability by creating
failover replicas, and you can schedule backups

00:00:26.189 --> 00:00:28.449
and maintenance windows as well.

00:00:28.449 --> 00:00:31.929
Click "Create," and in a few minutes your
instance should be created.

00:00:31.929 --> 00:00:34.660
You can see all your instance details on this
page.

00:00:34.660 --> 00:00:39.600
Now, go to the access control tab and then
go to the users tab.

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:41.390
Specify a new root password.

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:45.370
In your app.yaml, create an environment variable
section with the following information.

00:00:45.370 --> 00:00:49.120
Use the instance connection name from the
overview page and specify a database name

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:50.240
and root password.

00:00:50.240 --> 00:00:54.149
It's important to create a database and table
before using it with App Engine.

00:00:54.149 --> 00:00:57.930
You can use these environment variables to
connect to your Cloud SQL database just like

00:00:57.930 --> 00:00:59.379
any other MySQL database.

